### limit.onIdle(options?)

Returns a promise that resolves when the limiter is idle.

The limiter is idle when there are no running (or reserved) promises and nothing is waiting in the queue. Calling this on an already idle limiter resolves normally.

This is useful for tests and for shutting down cleanly, for example before flushing logs or closing resources.

```js
import pLimit from 'p-limit';

const limit = pLimit(1);

limit(() => doSomething());
limit(() => doSomethingElse());

await limit.onIdle();
console.log('All work is done');
```

#### options

Type: `object`

##### signal

Type: [`AbortSignal`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortSignal)

An `AbortSignal` that rejects the returned promise with the signal's `reason`.

Aborting only rejects that waiter. It does not cancel running or queued jobs, nor does it affect other `onIdle` waiters. A pre-aborted signal rejects even if the limiter is already idle. The abort listener is removed once the waiter settles.

```js
import pLimit from 'p-limit';

const limit = pLimit(1);
const controller = new AbortController();

limit(() => doSomething());

try {
	await limit.onIdle({signal: controller.signal});
} catch (error) {
	console.log('Stopped waiting for idle', error);
}
```

